+# Evaluation — OWASP Agent Control Standard (ACS) as declarative control vocabulary for SupremeAI's agent plane
+
+**Ticket:** #1739 · **Lane:** docs/process (scout envelope, zero code edits) · **Date:** 2026-10-29
+**Evaluated:** Agent Control Standard (ACS) v0.1.0, OWASP Gen AI Security Project (donated 2026-08-27, published 2026-09-01, Apache-2.0)
+**Sources (live, this cycle):** agentcontrolstandard.org; github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard (specification/ tree, README, reference-implementations/)
+**Verdict up front: `adopt` — as vocabulary first, machinery second.** SupremeAI's agent plane already implements most of ACS's three-layer model in scattered, unnamed form; aligning the names costs near-zero and exposes exactly two real gaps (pre-action intercept, live capability inventory). This document IS the deliverable the ticket asks for.
+
+---
+
+## 1. What ACS actually is (grounded, not hype)
+
+ACS is a **wire specification** for runtime agent control, not a framework or product. Three documents compose it:
+
+- **Instrument** — platforms expose standardized middleware hooks at every agent decision point; the repo enumerates **nineteen native lifecycle hooks** (input, output, tool-call request/response, planning transitions, memory operations, lifecycle). The wire is JSON-RPC 2.0: a `steps/toolCallRequest` notification carries `acs_version`, `request_id`, `metadata.agent_id`/`session_id`, and the `payload` (tool name + arguments); the enforcement side answers a **final verdict object**: `{"type":"final","decision":"allow|deny|modify","reasoning":"...","modifications":{"parameter_overrides":{...}}}`.
+- **Trace** — structured observability via OpenTelemetry semantic conventions (spans for the hook steps), OCSF event mapping for SIEM ingestion, export via `ACS_OTEL_FILE`/`ACS_OTEL_ENDPOINT`.
+- **Inspect** — Agent Bill of Materials (AgBOM): a **live** inventory of an agent's models/tools/dependencies, serializable as CycloneDX/SPDX/SWID, updating as agents discover tools or connect MCP servers.
+
+Enforcement is three-layered: **Tier 1** platform hooks (one-time platform ask), **Tier 2** a framework-agnostic SDK evaluating **declarative policies** (deterministic engines named in the spec: OPA/Rego or Cedar), **Tier 3** enterprise classifiers plugging into the same hooks. A **Guardian Agent** inspects what an agent is about to do and returns the verdict — inline, before the action reaches production. The spec explicitly positions itself against MCP/A2A ("the industry standardized how agents communicate; not how to control them") and against post-hoc observability ("tells you what an agent did after the fact").
+
+Honest maturity note: v0.1.0, spec-first (prose + JSON Schema), single reference implementation generation, donated to OWASP under three months ago. This is an early-standard adoption call, not a mature-dependency call.
+
